  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 147 PM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nAt 1 PM CST, it was sunny. Temperatures were warming through the\nupper 40s and 50s, and in to the 60s at some locations. South winds\n10 to 25 mph with gusts up to 35 mph were happening.\n\nThere could be some more fog late tonight, mainly across portions of \nnorth central South Dakota. Tonight's limiting factor will be \ncoverage and duration of mid/high clouds forecast to be working over \nthe CWA during the overnight hours. This cloud-cover will throw a \nblanket on really good fog formation/radiative cooling processes. \nCollaboration this morning yields patchy fog mention for tonight. \nLow confidence in fog developing, though.\n\nThe breezy/windy south-component winds of 15 to 30 mph with gusts \nover 40 mph at times look to persist Tuesday and Wednesday, helping \nto keep temperatures above normal. Still could be dealing with some \nrecord or near record warm low temperatures over the next couple of \nnights. On Wednesday into Wednesday night, the wind eventually takes \non more of a west-component as the cold air advection component of \nthe mid-week system kicks in. Cooler air (closer to normal) then \nsits in place over the region Thursday into Thursday night before \nlow level WAA develops by Friday morning ahead of the late week \nsystem.\n\nThe probability of more than a tenth of an inch of WAA-forced \nprecipitation Friday/Friday night is currently at ~55 to 85 percent. \nThe range of potential outcomes on the back-side of this late week \nsystem does include scenarios where it would be cold enough to snow. \nIt's just a matter of whether or not TROWAL-forced precipitation \nwill develop/work over the northern tier counties of the CWA heading \ninto Saturday. At this point, not much fan-fare over snow \naccumulation potential. Seems like the majority of the precipitation \nis falling as rain with this system. At least, that's how it looks \nin the range of outcomes right now.",
